<Article
  title="Water from the air, bottled in the room"
  description="H20 puts an atmospheric water generator on the shell and a micro bottling line inside. The unit comes later and still needs further research."
  kicker="Water"
  date="2026-09-30"
>
  <p>An atmospheric water generator condenses moisture from the air. On this shell it is a low unit on the roof, sized to the room, not a municipal plant. Inside, a short line fills plastic jugs.</p>
  <h2>What it is for</h2>
  <p>A site with humidity and power can make drinking water without a well. H20 is that idea on this shell, and it comes later. The generator and the line still need further research and development. Homes and the farm units are what you can ask about now. The amount of water, when the machine exists, is a function of humidity, temperature, and electricity. Dry winter air makes less. Any page that quotes litres per day without the weather is guessing, so this one does not.</p>
  <h2>The jugs</h2>
  <p>The jugs are plastic, because that is the container the network is designed around: light, familiar, and able to carry a label. A label can be blank when you are bottling for yourself. An advertiser’s label is the later model, described with the <a href="/journal/free-food-and-water">food and water network</a>. Water that leaves the shell for other people still has to meet the rules of the place it is filled. The machine does not replace that.</p>
  <h2>The room around the line</h2>
  <p>The line is meant to leave the shell livable. It is not a factory floor. Glass, oak, and the meadow stay. See the arrangement on <a href="/water">the water page</a>, and the shell it belongs to on <a href="/#shell">the room</a>.</p>
</Article>
